exports[`a personal VM names reachable peers neutrally from the record and requires the Operator's confirmation 1`] = `
"# This Environment
<!-- base-instructions-97; generated by Lazurio, do not edit: an edited file blocks every re-render of the Folder (a profile change, a refresh, a product upgrade) until it is restored; keep your own notes outside the generated files; {"preset":"hosted-personal"} -->
## Identity, from the recorded handover
- Name: \`example\` (personal-vm).
- Owner: the user with GitHub login \`example\` (id 12345).
- Tailnet: Headscale node \`example\`.
- Host: provider-estate \`example-estate\`, a higher administration and recovery domain than this Environment.

## Preset \`hosted-personal\` (version 1)
A user's one personal Remote Environment (decision 0153). Personalspace is present and intimate; a Buddy is an optional resident of this same Environment, not a second host. No Organization repositories are mounted: Organization work happens in Environments the Organization owns. The user's own sign-ins are used, and applications are owned by the OS service manager.

## Zones: what may reach what (decision 0155)
One user's Environments and clients form two zones. The **personal zone** holds the user's personal clients (laptop, phone) and their one personal Remote Environment; its Owner is the user. The **work zone** holds the work Remote Environments assigned to this user (possibly several, across Organizations of the same Conglomerate) and a work laptop issued by an Organization; its Owner is the Organization. The zone follows \`owner\` and \`operator\`, both bound to immutable GitHub IDs, never a Headscale user or a name.

- Inside a zone, both directions: personal client ↔ personal Remote Environment; work laptop ↔ work Remote Environment of the same Organization assigned to the same user. Work Remote Environments have no grant between each other, not even within one Organization.
- From personal to work: yes. Personal clients and the personal Remote Environment reach every work Remote Environment of the same user (SSH and HTTPS).
- From work to personal: never. No work Remote Environment or work laptop has a grant to a personal Remote Environment or a personal laptop.
- A shared Team Remote Environment accepts only inbound connections from members of the live GitHub Team it is bound to (decisions 0147/0149) and never has an outbound grant.
- Nothing implicitly between users. A phone is not an SSH server.
- Everything holds inside one Conglomerate (one Headscale tailnet); a personal Remote Environment is a node of its home tailnet only, and no bridge is built between tailnets.
- Administration of the Conglomerate Host belongs to the Owner of the Organization that owns it, only from their personal laptop and personal Remote Environment.

**This Environment's zone.** This is the personal Remote Environment of the personal zone. It may reach every work Remote Environment of its user; no work Environment may reach it.

## Boundaries in this Environment
- Personalspace: \`personalspace/\` is the intimate space of one user, the Owner of this Environment, and their optional Buddy. Nobody else reads it and it is never shared.
- Organizations: no Organization repositories are mounted in a personal Remote Environment. Organization work happens in Environments the Organization owns (see below).
- Work in another Environment: when you work for the user over SSH in an Organization's Environment, its boundaries and its \`AGENTS.md\` apply there, not these. Its browser and files belong to the Organization: do not take the user's personal matters or their sign-ins there.
- Identity: the user's own sign-ins. GitHub is the authority for source code (repositories, review, Publication), and Headscale holds the network. Entry to Environments is to be held by the Lazurio account (the target of root decision 0192); until that move is done, the existing path decides, a Team Environment's GitHub Team, say (\`manual/lazurio.md\`).
- Network: this Environment's network is one for every thread and session here, and Lazurio Machines manages its Tailscale. Never switch it, sign it out, point it at another login server or stop it: you would cut everyone else off. Reach another tailnet only the way the Environment offers, such as an approved headless client's socket (the section Adding a computer to a tailnet below) or that tailnet's own network space chosen for each command; when it offers none, tell the user (root decision 0217, addendum 2026-10-31).

## Adding a computer to a tailnet
Establish the user, device and target tailnet, then choose its stable name. Add the computer to the user's existing account; a second computer creates neither another account nor another Environment. Run client commands on the source computer. If you run in a hosted Environment, prepare the procedure for the agent on the source computer; do not rename this Environment.
1. Verify the account identity and Headscale login server from signed-in Lazurio or the administrator of that tailnet. Do not derive the login from the OS account, email or target Environment. For a linked GitHub account use the lowercase login frozen when its personal Headscale user was created; without GitHub use its existing label. Do not link GitHub for a name. If the identity is unknown, find it or ask for it first.
2. Name a new computer \`<login>-<device>\`, for example \`example-user-macbook\` or \`example-user-windows\`. Use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, without emoji or defaults such as \`node\`, \`localhost\` or a random OS name. Choose the device label once and keep it across tailnets; distinguish two devices of the same kind with stable labels such as \`macbook-air\` and \`macbook-pro\`. Do not add an Organization or Conglomerate to the device name: the verified login server and DNS suffix distinguish the network. A name is neither identity nor permission. The \`-personal-vm\` suffix belongs to a hosted personal Environment, not a laptop.
3. Before enrollment inspect the existing client: \`tailscale debug prefs\` (\`ControlURL\`) and \`tailscale status --json\`. Do not switch, log out or reset a different active tailnet yourself. If an approved headless client exists for the target network, use its socket in every command (\`tailscale --socket=<socket> …\`). Do not create another client just for this task. A headless client of the same computer is named \`<login>-<device>-cli\`, at most one per computer and tailnet, with its own approval.
4. On first connection pass an explicit \`--hostname=<name>\` with the verified \`--login-server=<address>\` through the client's supported sign-in procedure. The user completes OIDC sign-in with their Lazurio account; if supported sign-in is unavailable, hand off to the administrator instead of improvising legacy enrollment. For a newly enrolled, still unapproved device with a wrong name, run \`tailscale set --hostname=<name>\` on the device; preserve its other settings. If the macOS CLI is outside PATH, use the client supplied by the installed app. Do not install a second daemon or change the OS computer name for this. Never manually run \`headscale nodes rename\` on the Conglomerate Host.
5. Verify the result: \`ControlURL\` matches the target network and \`Self.DNSName\` from \`tailscale status --json\` has exactly the chosen first label and the correct suffix. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback: node ID, machine key, final name, owner and IPv4/IPv6. A \`-1\` suffix or another name is not success: resolve a collision or an earlier manual rename with the administrator; do not delete another node. Do not rename an approved device just for convention: its name is part of exact access bindings, so a change needs a coordinated plan and new verification. Treat re-enrollment changing the node ID or machine key the same way.
6. Tailnet registration grants no access yet. For a work or Team Environment, the Organization Admin approves the exact device and its assigned path through the network administrator's managed procedure. No Organization Admin approves a device for a personal Environment: a reviewed change by the Conglomerate Host owner admits it in the home tailnet. Each computer has its own binding and SSH key; never copy a private key. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback from step 5 for both computers and their exact network bindings; do not assume a device list in the Organization overview. Only after approval and deployment, if the target Environment offers SSH, continue in its Settings → SSH from your devices, pin its host key and verify \`ssh <alias> true\`. A personal Environment without tailnet or SSH, entered only by signing in with the Lazurio account on the web, has no such path. Distinguish registration, approval, deployment and the test; say what is missing.

## How the user works with you
The user sits at their own computer and works with you in the clients they prefer, possibly several at once: ChatGPT Desktop (formerly Codex Desktop) over SSH, T3 Code on the web or in its desktop app, and Lazurio MausBot. Per the handover, the user connects here over SSH. Your harness tells you which client you run in; the user may be working in another one meanwhile. Your shell, files and processes are here, not on the user's computer.

- **Previews.** \`localhost\` and \`127.0.0.1\` exist only here; never write a bare link to them into the chat.
  - When you run in ChatGPT Desktop over SSH, open work in progress in its built-in browser. Current versions open an SSH tunnel to the port themselves, on a random port of the user's computer. OpenAI does not document this and older versions cannot do it, so check that the page really loaded.
  - When you run in T3 Code or Lazurio MausBot, no port reaches the user. Start a module's application in progress (\`lazurio module start\`) and send its \`runtime.url\`; hand over any other page as a file.
- **Links.** \`lazurio chat link\` prints the link into this Environment's T3 Code.
- **Files from the user.** A file dragged into the ChatGPT Desktop chat arrives in \`~/.codex/attachments/\`; into T3 Code and Lazurio MausBot the user uploads it in the chat. Save what you will need later in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`.
- **Files for the user.** Save finished files in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`. The user has no direct access to this Environment's files and a \`/home/…\` path opens nothing on their computer, so hand over the result the way their client can: show an image in the chat, open a document in ChatGPT Desktop's preview. A large file (tens of MB) does not reliably pass through the preview; say so and offer a smaller or split version.
- **Chats.** Conversations from every client are stored here. The user deletes and archives chats in their own client; do not delete session files yourself. In ChatGPT Desktop, a thread's menu (right-click the thread in the sidebar) offers Permanently delete only in Codex mode (the switcher at the top left); in ChatGPT mode it offers only Archive. When someone asks how to delete a thread, tell them.

## Enabled tools
The CLIs of the Lazurio catalog that agents in this Environment are to use: the required ones and those the user enabled in this Folder (\`lazurio tools list\`, \`lazurio tools enable|disable <tool>\`). Enabling is context, not authority or installation: it grants no access, installs nothing and pins no version. Report a missing or signed-out tool to the user and install or sign it in only with their consent; offer them a newer version (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).

- \`gh\` (required): GitHub CLI for work with repositories, pull requests, issues and reviews. Use \`gh\` for all GitHub work. Verify the signed-in identity with \`gh auth status\` before a connected operation; a sign-in is not proof of the right to an exact operation.
- \`executor\` (required): This Environment's MCP gateway for directly connected Integrations and custom MCP servers; it runs as a service on localhost only and Lazurio looks after it. Use the Integrations connected directly through the MCP server \`executor\` (its tools \`skills\` and \`execute\`) or with \`executor call tools <integration> <owner> <connection> <tool> '<json>'\`; \`executor tools integrations\` and \`executor tools search "<task>"\` show what is there. Check the service and the agents' connection with \`lazurio executor status\`; when it reports that Executor is not running or not finished, tell the user: on their instruction \`lazurio executor setup\` repairs it (Settings → Tools → executor offers the same). An externally visible write through an Integration (sending, publishing, deleting) is a Publication: prepare the work and leave it unfinished; the user's explicit “Publish” for it is the full mandate to finish it (\`manual/working-here.md\`).

### Integrations and MCP
The Integrations (connected apps) and custom MCP servers of this Environment are held by the Environment's Executor and by Composio with the user's account; \`lazurio integrations list --json\` prints what is connected and which way, and the rules are in \`manual/working-here.md\` (section Integrations). Integrations are never recorded in the Folder, and neither a connected app nor an available MCP server is consent to Publication.

## Relationships
This Environment's tailnet peers as the handover records them from the Conglomerate Host grants (this Environment is in the personal zone): what this Environment may reach and what may reach it, per peer with its kind, zone, Organization, SSH host, account and direction, and the HTTPS gateway hostnames reachable from here.
- \`example-laptop\` (client device, personal zone): SSH both ways with \`example-laptop.tailnet.example.invalid\`; no HTTPS.
- \`example-workspace\` (work Remote Environment, work zone, Organization \`example\`): SSH from here to \`example-workspace.tailnet.example.invalid\` as \`operator\`; HTTPS \`launchpad.example-workspace.example.lazurio.io\`.
Lazurio enforces none of this: the Headscale policy of the Conglomerate Host does, and transport is not authentication, so SSH keys and application sign-in stay separate.

## Connecting a computer and a ChatGPT Desktop project
The agent on the user's computer sets up the connection, not you. You are in the Environment the computer connects to, and the computer's private key does not belong here.
The handover gives no Launchpad address, so do not guess it. Ask the Environment's administrator to add it.
After a successful SSH test, the agent on the source computer ends its chat reply with a clickable Markdown link to ChatGPT Desktop taken exactly from the page. It does not add the project itself or bypass app security restrictions: the user clicks. Explain that if the first click adds no project, they can click the same link once again; this sometimes happens and is not a required double-click. Once successful, the user sees the Lazurio folder in the left sidebar. A single Organization project may simply be named after the Organization; distinguish several Environments of one Organization by kind as well, such as work and Team, and give a personal project its own personal label. Only the display name changes, never the SSH alias or remote path. If the link still fails, guide the user through adding the page's exact values manually in Settings → Connections → SSH.
A registered key gives no network access by itself. The network administrator decides which devices reach the network (Headscale and the SSH grant). Do not switch the Tailscale profile on the user's computer.

## SSH to other Environments and devices
- Connect only to a peer's tailnet hostname as the handover records it (the \`ssh.host\` of a peer above), never to a bare tailnet address such as \`100.64.0.x\`: those addresses repeat in every tailnet and mean a different peer in each.
- Before connecting, verify the active tailnet: \`tailscale status --json\` → \`CurrentTailnet.Name\` must be this Environment's home tailnet and \`CurrentTailnet.MagicDNSSuffix\` must match the peers' recorded \`ssh.host\` names. If it does not, or you cannot tell, stop and tell the user. Never switch the Tailscale profile: it is one global setting for every session in this Environment (the section Boundaries in this Environment above).
- The peer's host key is always pinned: in \`~/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio\` under the peer's name as \`HostKeyAlias\`. With the values from the handover:

  \`\`\`sh
  PEER='peer-name'                  # the peer's name
  SSH_HOST='peer-name.tailnet.host' # its ssh.host
  TARGET="$SSH_HOST"               # or "account@$SSH_HOST" when the handover names ssh.user
  ssh -o HostKeyAlias="$PEER" -o UserKnownHostsFile="$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio" -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes "$TARGET"
  \`\`\`

- The pinned key comes from whoever owns the peer (the records of the Organization that owns it, the user for their own device), never from the first connection: no \`ssh-keyscan\`, no accepting an unknown key, never the general \`~/.ssh/known_hosts\`. Without a pinned key, do not connect; ask the user for it.
- “Host key changed” (\`REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED\`) first means “a different active tailnet”, never a reason to remove or accept the key again. Stop and report it.
- A listed peer is transport Headscale allows, not permission for the task: the SSH key, the account and the user's mandate stay separate.
- Lazurio enforces nothing about what this Environment can reach: the Headscale policy of the Conglomerate Host does. This manual only repeats what the handover recorded; never infer a peer the handover does not list from names or addresses.

## From this personal Remote Environment
Peers this Environment may reach over SSH, exactly as the handover records them:
- \`example-laptop\` (client device, personal zone): SSH both ways with \`example-laptop.tailnet.example.invalid\`; no HTTPS.
- \`example-workspace\` (work Remote Environment, work zone, Organization \`example\`): SSH from here to \`example-workspace.tailnet.example.invalid\` as \`operator\`; HTTPS \`launchpad.example-workspace.example.lazurio.io\`.
- Reachability is decided by Headscale and is neither identity nor mandate: the handover does not record whose a peer is. Before you work on a peer or operate a device, confirm with the user that it is theirs or assigned to them.
- Organization work (code, repositories, runs) belongs in a work Remote Environment the user confirms is assigned to them, never in this Environment: connect over SSH and work there. Never clone an Organization repository into this personal Remote Environment.
- What needs the user's own device, for example the signed-in browser, is done over SSH on a device the user confirms is theirs.
- No work Environment ever reaches this personal Remote Environment (decision 0155); do not open a way back.
"
`;

exports[`a personal VM names reachable peers neutrally from the record and requires the Operator's confirmation 2`] = `
"# Tenhle Environment
<!-- base-instructions-97; generováno Lazuriem, needituj: upravený soubor zablokuje každé nové vykreslení Folderu (změnu profilu, refresh i upgrade produktu), dokud se nevrátí do původní podoby; vlastní poznámky drž mimo generované soubory; {"preset":"hosted-personal"} -->
## Identita podle zaznamenaného handoveru
- Název: \`example\` (personal-vm).
- Owner: uživatel s GitHub loginem \`example\` (id 12345).
- Tailnet: Headscale node \`example\`.
- Host: provider-estate \`example-estate\`, vyšší doména správy a obnovy než tenhle Environment.

## Preset \`hosted-personal\` (verze 1)
Jediný osobní Remote Environment uživatele (decision 0153). Personalspace je přítomný a intimní; Buddy je volitelný rezident téhož Environmentu, ne druhý host. Žádná repa Organizací tu nejsou namountovaná: práce v Organizaci probíhá na Environmentech, které Organizace vlastní. Používají se vlastní přihlášení uživatele a aplikace vlastní OS service manager.

## Zóny: co kam smí (decision 0155)
Environmenty a klienti jednoho uživatele tvoří dvě zóny. **Osobní zóna** zahrnuje osobní klienty uživatele (laptop, telefon) a jeho jediný osobní Remote Environment; jejím Ownerem je uživatel. **Pracovní zóna** zahrnuje pracovní Remote Environmenty přiřazené tomuhle uživateli (klidně několik, napříč Organizacemi téhož Conglomerate) a pracovní laptop vydaný Organizací; jejím Ownerem je Organizace. Zóna se řídí poli \`owner\` a \`operator\`, obě svázaná s neměnnými GitHub ID, nikdy Headscale uživatelem ani jménem.

- Uvnitř zóny oběma směry: osobní klient ↔ osobní Remote Environment; pracovní laptop ↔ pracovní Remote Environment téže Organizace přiřazený témuž uživateli. Pracovní Remote Environmenty mezi sebou žádný grant nemají, ani v rámci jedné Organizace.
- Z osobní do pracovní: ano. Osobní klienti a osobní Remote Environment smějí na každý pracovní Remote Environment téhož uživatele (SSH i HTTPS).
- Z pracovní do osobní: nikdy. Žádný pracovní Remote Environment ani pracovní laptop nemá grant na osobní Remote Environment ani osobní laptop.
- Sdílený týmový Remote Environment přijímá jen příchozí spojení od členů živého GitHub Teamu, ke kterému je svázaný (decisions 0147/0149), a nikdy nemá odchozí grant.
- Mezi uživateli nic implicitně. Telefon není SSH server.
- Všechno platí uvnitř jednoho Conglomerate (jednoho Headscale tailnetu); osobní Remote Environment je node jen svého domovského tailnetu a mezi tailnety se žádný most nestaví.
- Správa Conglomerate Hostu patří Ownerovi Organizace, která ho vlastní, a jen z jeho osobního laptopu a osobního Remote Environmentu.

**Zóna tohohle Environmentu.** Tenhle Environment je osobní Remote Environment osobní zóny. Smí na každý pracovní Remote Environment svého uživatele; žádný pracovní Environment na něj nesmí.

## Hranice na tomhle Environmentu
- Personalspace: \`personalspace/\` je intimní prostor jednoho uživatele, Ownera tohohle Environmentu, a jeho volitelného Buddyho. Nikdo cizí ho nečte a nikdy se nesdílí.
- Organizace: na osobním Remote Environmentu nejsou namountovaná žádná repa Organizací. Práce v Organizaci probíhá na Environmentech, které Organizace vlastní (viz níže).
- Práce v jiném Environmentu: když pro uživatele pracuješ přes SSH v Environmentu Organizace, platí tam jeho hranice a jeho \`AGENTS.md\`, ne tyhle. Jeho prohlížeč a soubory patří Organizaci: osobní věci uživatele ani jejich přihlášení do nich nenos.
- Identita: vlastní přihlášení uživatele. GitHub je autoritou zdrojového kódu (repozitáře, review, Publikace), síť drží Headscale. Vstup do Environmentů má cílově držet Lazurio účet (root decision 0192); dokud přechod není hotový, rozhoduje dosavadní cesta, třeba GitHub Team týmového Environmentu (\`manual/lazurio.md\`).
- Síť: síť tohoto Environmentu je jedna pro všechna vlákna a relace tady; jeho Tailscale spravuje Lazurio Machines. Nikdy ji nepřepínej, neodhlašuj, nepřesměrovávej na jiný login server ani nevypínej: odřízl bys tím všechny ostatní. Do jiného tailnetu choď jen cestou, kterou Environment nabízí, třeba přes socket schváleného headless klienta (oddíl Přidání počítače do tailnetu níže) nebo přes vlastní síťový prostor toho tailnetu, který volíš u každého příkazu; když žádnou nenabízí, řekni to uživateli (root rozhodnutí 0217, dodatek 2026-10-31).

## Přidání počítače do tailnetu
Nejdřív urči uživatele, zařízení a cílový tailnet, potom jeho stálé jméno. Počítač se přidává ke stávajícímu účtu uživatele; druhý počítač nevytváří další účet ani další Prostředí. Agente, příkazy pro klienta spouštěj na zdrojovém počítači. Pokud běžíš v hostovaném Prostředí, připrav postup pro agenta na zdrojovém počítači; nepřejmenovávej toto Prostředí.
1. Ověř identitu účtu a Headscale login server z přihlášeného Lazuria nebo od správce daného tailnetu. Login neodvozuj ze jména OS účtu, e-mailu ani cílového Environmentu. U propojeného GitHub účtu použij lowercase login zmrazený při založení osobního Headscale usera; u účtu bez GitHubu použij jeho existující štítek. GitHub kvůli názvu nepropojuj. Když identitu neznáš, nejdřív ji dohledej nebo si ji vyžádej.
2. Nový počítač pojmenuj \`<login>-<zařízení>\`, například \`example-user-macbook\` nebo \`example-user-windows\`. Použij malá písmena, číslice a spojovníky, bez emoji a výchozích jmen \`node\`, \`localhost\` či náhodného názvu OS. Štítek zařízení zvol jednou a drž ho ve všech tailnetech; pro dvě zařízení stejného druhu zvol stálé rozlišení, například \`macbook-air\` a \`macbook-pro\`. Organizaci ani Conglomerate do jména zařízení nepřidávej: síť rozlišuje ověřený login server a DNS suffix. Jméno není identita ani oprávnění. Koncovka \`-personal-vm\` patří osobnímu hostovanému Environmentu, ne notebooku.
3. Před registrací zkontroluj existujícího klienta: \`tailscale debug prefs\` (\`ControlURL\`) a \`tailscale status --json\`. Nesprávný aktivní tailnet sám nepřepínej, neodhlašuj jej a neresetuj jeho stav. Existuje-li pro cílovou síť schválený headless klient, použij jeho socket ve všech příkazech (\`tailscale --socket=<socket> …\`). Nového dalšího klienta nevytvářej jen pro tento úkol. Headless klient stejného počítače má jméno \`<login>-<zařízení>-cli\`, nejvýš jeden na počítač a tailnet, a vlastní schválení.
4. Při prvním připojení nastav explicitní \`--hostname=<jméno>\` spolu s ověřeným \`--login-server=<adresa>\` v podporovaném přihlašovacím postupu klienta. OIDC přihlášení dokončí uživatel svým Lazurio účtem; pokud podporované přihlášení chybí, předej to správci místo improvizované staré registrace. U nově zaregistrovaného, dosud neschváleného zařízení se špatným jménem použij na zařízení \`tailscale set --hostname=<jméno>\`; ostatní nastavení zachovej. Má-li macOS CLI mimo PATH, použij klienta dodaného nainstalovanou aplikací. Kvůli názvu neinstaluj druhý daemon a neměň systémové jméno počítače. Na Conglomerate Hostu ručně nepoužívej \`headscale nodes rename\`.
5. Ověř výsledek: \`ControlURL\` odpovídá cílové síti a \`Self.DNSName\` z \`tailscale status --json\` má přesný zvolený první štítek a správný suffix. Správce porovná čerstvý Headscale readback: node ID, machine key, konečné jméno, vlastníka a IPv4/IPv6. Přípona \`-1\` nebo jiné jméno není úspěch: dořeš kolizi nebo dřívější ruční přejmenování se správcem, cizí uzel nemaž. Už schválené zařízení nepřejmenovávej jen kvůli konvenci: jméno je součástí přesných vazeb přístupu, takže jeho změna potřebuje koordinovaný plán a nové ověření. Stejně postupuj při novém přihlášení, které změní node ID nebo machine key.
6. Registrace do tailnetu ještě přístup neuděluje. Pro Pracovní nebo Týmové Prostředí schvaluje Admin Organizace konkrétní zařízení a jeho přidělenou cestu v řízeném postupu správce sítě. Do Osobního Prostředí zařízení Admin Organizace neschvaluje: vstup v domovském tailnetu povolí reviewovaná změna vlastníka Conglomerate Hostu. Každý počítač má vlastní vazbu a vlastní SSH klíč; privátní klíč se nekopíruje. Správce porovná čerstvý Headscale readback z kroku 5 pro oba počítače a jejich přesné síťové vazby; nepředpokládej seznam zařízení v Přehledu Organizace. Až po schválení a nasazení, pokud cílové Prostředí nabízí SSH, pokračuj v jeho Nastavení → SSH ze zařízení, připni klíč hostu a ověř \`ssh <alias> true\`. Osobní Prostředí bez tailnetu a SSH, do kterého se vstupuje jen přihlášením Lazurio účtem z webu, tento postup nemá. Rozlišuj registraci, schválení, nasazení a test; řekni, co chybí.

## Jak s tebou uživatel pracuje
Uživatel sedí u svého počítače a pracuje s tebou v klientech, které mu vyhovují, klidně ve více zároveň: v ChatGPT Desktopu (dřív Codex Desktop) přes SSH, v T3 Code na webu nebo v desktopové aplikaci a v Lazurio MausBotu. Podle handoveru se sem uživatel připojuje přes SSH. Ve kterém klientovi právě běžíš ty, poznáš podle svého harnessu; uživatel mezitím může pracovat i v jiném. Tvůj shell, soubory a procesy jsou tady, ne u uživatele.

- **Náhledy.** \`localhost\` a \`127.0.0.1\` existují jen tady; holý odkaz na ně do chatu nepiš.
  - Běžíš-li v ChatGPT Desktopu přes SSH, otevři rozpracovanou stránku v jeho integrovaném prohlížeči. Aktuální verze si k portu samy otevřou SSH tunel na náhodný port počítače uživatele. OpenAI to nedokumentuje a starší verze to neumí, proto ověř, že se stránka opravdu načetla.
  - Běžíš-li v T3 Code nebo Lazurio MausBotu, port se k uživateli nepřesměruje. Rozpracovanou aplikaci modulu spusť (\`lazurio module start\`) a pošli její \`runtime.url\`; jinou stránku předej jako soubor.
- **Odkazy.** \`lazurio chat link\` vypíše odkaz do T3 Code tohohle Environmentu.
- **Soubory od uživatele.** Soubor přetažený do chatu ChatGPT Desktopu dorazí do \`~/.codex/attachments/\`; do T3 Code a Lazurio MausBotu ho uživatel nahraje v chatu. Co budeš potřebovat i později, ulož do \`~/Documents/<úkol>/\`.
- **Soubory pro uživatele.** Hotové soubory ukládej do \`~/Documents/<úkol>/\`. Uživatel k souborům tohohle Environmentu nemá přímý přístup a cesta \`/home/…\` mu na jeho počítači nic neotevře, proto mu výsledek předej tím, co jeho klient umí: obrázek ukaž v chatu, dokument otevři v náhledu ChatGPT Desktopu. Velký soubor (desítky MB) náhled spolehlivě nepřenese; řekni to a nabídni menší nebo rozdělenou verzi.
- **Chaty.** Konverzace ze všech klientů se ukládají sem. Mazat a archivovat chaty umí uživatel sám ve svém klientovi; soubory relací sám nemaž. V ChatGPT Desktopu je v nabídce vlákna (pravé tlačítko na vláknu v postranním panelu) trvalé smazání (Permanently delete) jen v režimu Codex (přepínač vlevo nahoře); v režimu ChatGPT je tam jen archivace (Archive). Když se uživatel ptá, jak vlákno smazat, řekni mu to.

## Zapnuté nástroje
CLI z katalogu Lazuria, která mají agenti na tomhle Environmentu používat: povinná a ta, která uživatel v tomhle Folderu zapnul (\`lazurio tools list\`, \`lazurio tools enable|disable <tool>\`). Zapnutí je kontext, ne autorita ani instalace: neuděluje přístup, nic neinstaluje a nepinuje verzi. Chybějící nebo nepřihlášený nástroj nahlas uživateli a instaluj nebo přihlašuj ho jen s jeho souhlasem; novější verzi mu nabídni (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).

- \`gh\` (povinný): GitHub CLI pro práci s repozitáři, pull requesty, issues a review. Používej \`gh\` pro veškerou práci s GitHubem. Před připojenou operací ověř přihlášenou identitu příkazem \`gh auth status\`; přihlášení není důkaz práva k přesné operaci.
- \`executor\` (povinný): MCP brána tohoto Prostředí pro přímo připojené Integrace a vlastní MCP servery; běží jako služba jen na localhostu a stará se o ni Lazurio. Integrace připojené přímo používej přes MCP server \`executor\` (jeho nástroje \`skills\` a \`execute\`) nebo příkazem \`executor call tools <integrace> <vlastník> <připojení> <nástroj> '<json>'\`; co je k dispozici, ukáže \`executor tools integrations\` a \`executor tools search "<úkol>"\`. Stav služby a napojení agentů ověříš příkazem \`lazurio executor status\`; když hlásí, že Executor neběží nebo není hotový, řekni to uživateli: na jeho pokyn ho opraví \`lazurio executor setup\` (totéž nabízí Nastavení → Nástroje → executor). Zápis viditelný navenek přes Integraci (odeslání, zveřejnění, smazání) je Publikace: práci připrav a nech ji nedokončenou; výslovné „Publikuj“ uživatele k ní je plný mandát k jejímu dokončení (\`manual/working-here.md\`).

### Integrace a MCP
Integrace (připojené aplikace) a vlastní MCP servery tohohle Environmentu drží Executor Environmentu a Composio s účtem uživatele; co je připojené a kudy, vypíše \`lazurio integrations list --json\` a pravidla jsou v \`manual/working-here.md\` (oddíl Integrace). Do Folderu se Integrace nezapisují a připojená aplikace ani dostupný MCP server nejsou souhlas s Publikací.

## Vztahy
Peers tohohle Environmentu v tailnetu, jak je handover odvozuje z grantů Conglomerate Hostu (tenhle Environment je v osobní zóně): kam tenhle Environment smí a kdo smí na něj, u každého peeru jeho druh, zóna, Organizace, SSH host, účet a směr a HTTPS hostnames gateway dostupné odsud.
- \`example-laptop\` (klientské zařízení, osobní zóna): SSH oběma směry s \`example-laptop.tailnet.example.invalid\`; bez HTTPS.
- \`example-workspace\` (pracovní Remote Environment, pracovní zóna, Organizace \`example\`): SSH odsud na \`example-workspace.tailnet.example.invalid\` jako \`operator\`; HTTPS \`launchpad.example-workspace.example.lazurio.io\`.
Nic z toho Lazurio nevynucuje: vynucuje to Headscale policy Conglomerate Hostu a transport není autentizace, takže SSH klíče i přihlášení do aplikací zůstávají samostatné.

## Připojení počítače a projektu v ChatGPT Desktopu
Připojení nastavuje agent na počítači uživatele, ne ty. Jsi v Environmentu, ke kterému se počítač připojuje, a soukromý klíč počítače sem nepatří.
Handover neuvádí adresu Launchpadu, tak ji neodvozuj. Požádej správce Environmentu, ať ji doplní.
Agent na zdrojovém počítači po úspěšném testu SSH zakončí odpověď v chatu klikacím Markdown odkazem do ChatGPT Desktopu převzatým přesně ze stránky. Projekt sám nepřidává ani neobchází bezpečnostní omezení aplikace: klikne uživatel. Vysvětlí mu, že pokud první kliknutí projekt nepřidá, může na stejný odkaz kliknout ještě jednou; občas se to stává, není to povinný dvojklik. Po úspěchu uživatel vidí vlevo složku Lazurio. Jediný projekt Organizace může pojmenovat prostě názvem Organizace; více Prostředí téže Organizace rozliší i druhem, třeba Pracovní a Týmové, a osobní projekt vlastním osobním označením. Mění se jen zobrazovaný název, nikdy SSH alias ani vzdálená cesta. Pokud odkaz stále nefunguje, proveď uživatele ručním přidáním přesných hodnot ze stránky v Settings → Connections → SSH.
Zaregistrovaný klíč sám přístup do sítě nedává. Která zařízení se do sítě dostanou, rozhoduje správce sítě (Headscale a povolení SSH). Profil Tailscale na počítači uživatele nepřepínej.

## SSH na další Environmenty a zařízení
- Připojuj se jen na tailnet hostname peeru tak, jak ho uvádí handover (\`ssh.host\` u peerů výše), nikdy na holou tailnet adresu typu \`100.64.0.x\`: ty se opakují v každém tailnetu a v každém znamenají jiný peer.
- Před spojením ověř aktivní tailnet: \`tailscale status --json\` → \`CurrentTailnet.Name\` musí být domovský tailnet tohohle Environmentu a \`CurrentTailnet.MagicDNSSuffix\` musí odpovídat zaznamenaným \`ssh.host\` peerů. Pokud neodpovídá, nebo si nejsi jistý, zastav se a řekni to uživateli. Profil Tailscale nikdy nepřepínej: je to jedno globální nastavení pro všechny relace na tomhle Environmentu (oddíl Hranice na tomhle Environmentu výše).
- Host klíč peeru je vždy pinnutý: v souboru \`~/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio\` pod jménem peeru jako \`HostKeyAlias\`. S hodnotami z handoveru:

  \`\`\`sh
  PEER='peer-name'                  # jméno peeru
  SSH_HOST='peer-name.tailnet.host' # jeho ssh.host
  TARGET="$SSH_HOST"               # nebo "account@$SSH_HOST", když handover uvádí ssh.user
  ssh -o HostKeyAlias="$PEER" -o UserKnownHostsFile="$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio" -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes "$TARGET"
  \`\`\`

- Pinnutý klíč pochází od toho, kdo peer vlastní (z evidence Organizace, která ho vlastní, u vlastního zařízení od uživatele), nikdy z prvního spojení: žádný \`ssh-keyscan\`, žádné přijetí neznámého klíče a nikdy obecný \`~/.ssh/known_hosts\`. Bez pinnutého klíče se nepřipojuj a požádej o něj uživatele.
- Hláška „host key changed“ (\`REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED\`) znamená nejdřív „jiný aktivní tailnet“, nikdy důvod klíč smazat nebo znovu přijmout. Zastav se a nahlas to.
- Peer v seznamu znamená, že spojení povoluje Headscale, ne že ho smíš pro daný úkol použít: SSH klíč, účet i mandát uživatele zůstávají samostatné.
- Kam tenhle Environment dosáhne, Lazurio nevynucuje: vynucuje to Headscale policy Conglomerate Hostu. Tenhle manuál jen opisuje, co zaznamenal handover; peer, který handover neuvádí, neodvozuj ze jmen ani z adres.

## Z tohohle osobního Remote Environmentu
Peers, na které odsud smíš přes SSH, přesně jak je zaznamenal handover:
- \`example-laptop\` (klientské zařízení, osobní zóna): SSH oběma směry s \`example-laptop.tailnet.example.invalid\`; bez HTTPS.
- \`example-workspace\` (pracovní Remote Environment, pracovní zóna, Organizace \`example\`): SSH odsud na \`example-workspace.tailnet.example.invalid\` jako \`operator\`; HTTPS \`launchpad.example-workspace.example.lazurio.io\`.
- Dosažitelnost rozhoduje Headscale a není to identita ani mandát: handover nezaznamenává, čí peer je. Než na peeru začneš pracovat nebo ovládat zařízení, potvrď s uživatelem, že je jeho nebo že je přiřazený jemu.
- Práce v Organizaci (kód, repozitáře, běhy) patří na pracovní Remote Environment, který ti uživatel potvrdí jako přiřazený jemu, nikdy na tenhle Environment: připoj se přes SSH a pracuj tam. Repozitář Organizace sem nikdy neklonuj.
- Co potřebuje vlastní zařízení uživatele, například přihlášený prohlížeč, se dělá přes SSH na zařízení, které ti uživatel potvrdí jako své.
- Žádný pracovní Environment na tenhle osobní Remote Environment nikdy nesmí (decision 0155); cestu zpět neotevírej.
"
`;

exports[`rendered manual/this-machine.md snapshot: local 1`] = `
"# This Environment
<!-- base-instructions-97; generated by Lazurio, do not edit: an edited file blocks every re-render of the Folder (a profile change, a refresh, a product upgrade) until it is restored; keep your own notes outside the generated files; {"preset":"local"} -->
## Identity, from the recorded handover
- The user's own workstation. No handover was recorded; the signed-in user is also its Owner.

## Preset \`local\` (version 1)
The user's own workstation, without a handover. The signed-in user is also its Owner. Personalspace is present, Organization repositories live under \`organizations/<org>/\`, and the user's own sign-ins are used. Applications started from the Launchpad live only for its session.

## Zones: what may reach what (decision 0155)
One user's Environments and clients form two zones. The **personal zone** holds the user's personal clients (laptop, phone) and their one personal Remote Environment; its Owner is the user. The **work zone** holds the work Remote Environments assigned to this user (possibly several, across Organizations of the same Conglomerate) and a work laptop issued by an Organization; its Owner is the Organization. The zone follows \`owner\` and \`operator\`, both bound to immutable GitHub IDs, never a Headscale user or a name.

- Inside a zone, both directions: personal client ↔ personal Remote Environment; work laptop ↔ work Remote Environment of the same Organization assigned to the same user. Work Remote Environments have no grant between each other, not even within one Organization.
- From personal to work: yes. Personal clients and the personal Remote Environment reach every work Remote Environment of the same user (SSH and HTTPS).
- From work to personal: never. No work Remote Environment or work laptop has a grant to a personal Remote Environment or a personal laptop.
- A shared Team Remote Environment accepts only inbound connections from members of the live GitHub Team it is bound to (decisions 0147/0149) and never has an outbound grant.
- Nothing implicitly between users. A phone is not an SSH server.
- Everything holds inside one Conglomerate (one Headscale tailnet); a personal Remote Environment is a node of its home tailnet only, and no bridge is built between tailnets.
- Administration of the Conglomerate Host belongs to the Owner of the Organization that owns it, only from their personal laptop and personal Remote Environment.

**This Environment's zone.** A workstation is a client of its user. Its zone follows who owns it: a personal laptop is in the personal zone, a work laptop issued by an Organization is in that Organization's work zone. The Folder does not record which one this is.

## Boundaries in this Environment
- Personalspace: \`personalspace/\` is the intimate space of one user, the Owner of this Environment, and their optional Buddy. Nobody else reads it and it is never shared.
- Organizations: repositories live under \`organizations/<org>/\`; each Organization is its own access boundary and its own git repository with its own \`AGENTS.md\`.
- Work in another Environment: when you work for the user over SSH in an Organization's Environment, its boundaries and its \`AGENTS.md\` apply there, not these. Its browser and files belong to the Organization: do not take the user's personal matters or their sign-ins there.
- Identity: the user's own sign-ins. GitHub is the authority for source code (repositories, review, Publication), and Headscale holds the network. Entry to Environments is to be held by the Lazurio account (the target of root decision 0192); until that move is done, the existing path decides, a Team Environment's GitHub Team, say (\`manual/lazurio.md\`).
- Network: this Environment's network is one for every thread and session here. Never switch it, sign it out, point it at another login server or stop it: you would cut everyone else off. Reach another tailnet only the way the Environment offers, such as an approved headless client's socket (the section Adding a computer to a tailnet below) or that tailnet's own network space chosen for each command; when it offers none, tell the user (root decision 0217, addendum 2026-10-31).

## Adding a computer to a tailnet
Establish the user, device and target tailnet, then choose its stable name. Add the computer to the user's existing account; a second computer creates neither another account nor another Environment. Run client commands on the source computer. If you run in a hosted Environment, prepare the procedure for the agent on the source computer; do not rename this Environment.
1. Verify the account identity and Headscale login server from signed-in Lazurio or the administrator of that tailnet. Do not derive the login from the OS account, email or target Environment. For a linked GitHub account use the lowercase login frozen when its personal Headscale user was created; without GitHub use its existing label. Do not link GitHub for a name. If the identity is unknown, find it or ask for it first.
2. Name a new computer \`<login>-<device>\`, for example \`example-user-macbook\` or \`example-user-windows\`. Use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, without emoji or defaults such as \`node\`, \`localhost\` or a random OS name. Choose the device label once and keep it across tailnets; distinguish two devices of the same kind with stable labels such as \`macbook-air\` and \`macbook-pro\`. Do not add an Organization or Conglomerate to the device name: the verified login server and DNS suffix distinguish the network. A name is neither identity nor permission. The \`-personal-vm\` suffix belongs to a hosted personal Environment, not a laptop.
3. Before enrollment inspect the existing client: \`tailscale debug prefs\` (\`ControlURL\`) and \`tailscale status --json\`. Do not switch, log out or reset a different active tailnet yourself. If an approved headless client exists for the target network, use its socket in every command (\`tailscale --socket=<socket> …\`). Do not create another client just for this task. A headless client of the same computer is named \`<login>-<device>-cli\`, at most one per computer and tailnet, with its own approval.
4. On first connection pass an explicit \`--hostname=<name>\` with the verified \`--login-server=<address>\` through the client's supported sign-in procedure. The user completes OIDC sign-in with their Lazurio account; if supported sign-in is unavailable, hand off to the administrator instead of improvising legacy enrollment. For a newly enrolled, still unapproved device with a wrong name, run \`tailscale set --hostname=<name>\` on the device; preserve its other settings. If the macOS CLI is outside PATH, use the client supplied by the installed app. Do not install a second daemon or change the OS computer name for this. Never manually run \`headscale nodes rename\` on the Conglomerate Host.
5. Verify the result: \`ControlURL\` matches the target network and \`Self.DNSName\` from \`tailscale status --json\` has exactly the chosen first label and the correct suffix. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback: node ID, machine key, final name, owner and IPv4/IPv6. A \`-1\` suffix or another name is not success: resolve a collision or an earlier manual rename with the administrator; do not delete another node. Do not rename an approved device just for convention: its name is part of exact access bindings, so a change needs a coordinated plan and new verification. Treat re-enrollment changing the node ID or machine key the same way.
6. Tailnet registration grants no access yet. For a work or Team Environment, the Organization Admin approves the exact device and its assigned path through the network administrator's managed procedure. No Organization Admin approves a device for a personal Environment: a reviewed change by the Conglomerate Host owner admits it in the home tailnet. Each computer has its own binding and SSH key; never copy a private key. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback from step 5 for both computers and their exact network bindings; do not assume a device list in the Organization overview. Only after approval and deployment, if the target Environment offers SSH, continue in its Settings → SSH from your devices, pin its host key and verify \`ssh <alias> true\`. A personal Environment without tailnet or SSH, entered only by signing in with the Lazurio account on the web, has no such path. Distinguish registration, approval, deployment and the test; say what is missing.

## Enabled tools
The CLIs of the Lazurio catalog that agents in this Environment are to use: the required ones and those the user enabled in this Folder (\`lazurio tools list\`, \`lazurio tools enable|disable <tool>\`). Enabling is context, not authority or installation: it grants no access, installs nothing and pins no version. Report a missing or signed-out tool to the user and install or sign it in only with their consent; offer them a newer version (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).

- \`gh\` (required): GitHub CLI for work with repositories, pull requests, issues and reviews. Use \`gh\` for all GitHub work. Verify the signed-in identity with \`gh auth status\` before a connected operation; a sign-in is not proof of the right to an exact operation.

### Integrations and MCP
The Integrations (connected apps) and custom MCP servers of this Environment are held by the Environment's Executor and by Composio with the user's account; \`lazurio integrations list --json\` prints what is connected and which way, and the rules are in \`manual/working-here.md\` (section Integrations). Integrations are never recorded in the Folder, and neither a connected app nor an available MCP server is consent to Publication.
"
`;

exports[`rendered manual/this-machine.md snapshot: hosted-personal 1`] = `
"# This Environment
<!-- base-instructions-97; generated by Lazurio, do not edit: an edited file blocks every re-render of the Folder (a profile change, a refresh, a product upgrade) until it is restored; keep your own notes outside the generated files; {"preset":"hosted-personal"} -->
## Identity, from the recorded handover
- Name: \`example\` (personal-vm).
- Owner: the user with GitHub login \`example\` (id 12345).
- Tailnet: Headscale node \`example\`.
- Host: provider-estate \`example-estate\`, a higher administration and recovery domain than this Environment.

## Preset \`hosted-personal\` (version 1)
A user's one personal Remote Environment (decision 0153). Personalspace is present and intimate; a Buddy is an optional resident of this same Environment, not a second host. No Organization repositories are mounted: Organization work happens in Environments the Organization owns. The user's own sign-ins are used, and applications are owned by the OS service manager.

## Zones: what may reach what (decision 0155)
One user's Environments and clients form two zones. The **personal zone** holds the user's personal clients (laptop, phone) and their one personal Remote Environment; its Owner is the user. The **work zone** holds the work Remote Environments assigned to this user (possibly several, across Organizations of the same Conglomerate) and a work laptop issued by an Organization; its Owner is the Organization. The zone follows \`owner\` and \`operator\`, both bound to immutable GitHub IDs, never a Headscale user or a name.

- Inside a zone, both directions: personal client ↔ personal Remote Environment; work laptop ↔ work Remote Environment of the same Organization assigned to the same user. Work Remote Environments have no grant between each other, not even within one Organization.
- From personal to work: yes. Personal clients and the personal Remote Environment reach every work Remote Environment of the same user (SSH and HTTPS).
- From work to personal: never. No work Remote Environment or work laptop has a grant to a personal Remote Environment or a personal laptop.
- A shared Team Remote Environment accepts only inbound connections from members of the live GitHub Team it is bound to (decisions 0147/0149) and never has an outbound grant.
- Nothing implicitly between users. A phone is not an SSH server.
- Everything holds inside one Conglomerate (one Headscale tailnet); a personal Remote Environment is a node of its home tailnet only, and no bridge is built between tailnets.
- Administration of the Conglomerate Host belongs to the Owner of the Organization that owns it, only from their personal laptop and personal Remote Environment.

**This Environment's zone.** This is the personal Remote Environment of the personal zone. It may reach every work Remote Environment of its user; no work Environment may reach it.

## Boundaries in this Environment
- Personalspace: \`personalspace/\` is the intimate space of one user, the Owner of this Environment, and their optional Buddy. Nobody else reads it and it is never shared.
- Organizations: no Organization repositories are mounted in a personal Remote Environment. Organization work happens in Environments the Organization owns (see below).
- Work in another Environment: when you work for the user over SSH in an Organization's Environment, its boundaries and its \`AGENTS.md\` apply there, not these. Its browser and files belong to the Organization: do not take the user's personal matters or their sign-ins there.
- Identity: the user's own sign-ins. GitHub is the authority for source code (repositories, review, Publication), and Headscale holds the network. Entry to Environments is to be held by the Lazurio account (the target of root decision 0192); until that move is done, the existing path decides, a Team Environment's GitHub Team, say (\`manual/lazurio.md\`).
- Network: this Environment's network is one for every thread and session here, and Lazurio Machines manages its Tailscale. Never switch it, sign it out, point it at another login server or stop it: you would cut everyone else off. Reach another tailnet only the way the Environment offers, such as an approved headless client's socket (the section Adding a computer to a tailnet below) or that tailnet's own network space chosen for each command; when it offers none, tell the user (root decision 0217, addendum 2026-10-31).

## Adding a computer to a tailnet
Establish the user, device and target tailnet, then choose its stable name. Add the computer to the user's existing account; a second computer creates neither another account nor another Environment. Run client commands on the source computer. If you run in a hosted Environment, prepare the procedure for the agent on the source computer; do not rename this Environment.
1. Verify the account identity and Headscale login server from signed-in Lazurio or the administrator of that tailnet. Do not derive the login from the OS account, email or target Environment. For a linked GitHub account use the lowercase login frozen when its personal Headscale user was created; without GitHub use its existing label. Do not link GitHub for a name. If the identity is unknown, find it or ask for it first.
2. Name a new computer \`<login>-<device>\`, for example \`example-user-macbook\` or \`example-user-windows\`. Use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, without emoji or defaults such as \`node\`, \`localhost\` or a random OS name. Choose the device label once and keep it across tailnets; distinguish two devices of the same kind with stable labels such as \`macbook-air\` and \`macbook-pro\`. Do not add an Organization or Conglomerate to the device name: the verified login server and DNS suffix distinguish the network. A name is neither identity nor permission. The \`-personal-vm\` suffix belongs to a hosted personal Environment, not a laptop.
3. Before enrollment inspect the existing client: \`tailscale debug prefs\` (\`ControlURL\`) and \`tailscale status --json\`. Do not switch, log out or reset a different active tailnet yourself. If an approved headless client exists for the target network, use its socket in every command (\`tailscale --socket=<socket> …\`). Do not create another client just for this task. A headless client of the same computer is named \`<login>-<device>-cli\`, at most one per computer and tailnet, with its own approval.
4. On first connection pass an explicit \`--hostname=<name>\` with the verified \`--login-server=<address>\` through the client's supported sign-in procedure. The user completes OIDC sign-in with their Lazurio account; if supported sign-in is unavailable, hand off to the administrator instead of improvising legacy enrollment. For a newly enrolled, still unapproved device with a wrong name, run \`tailscale set --hostname=<name>\` on the device; preserve its other settings. If the macOS CLI is outside PATH, use the client supplied by the installed app. Do not install a second daemon or change the OS computer name for this. Never manually run \`headscale nodes rename\` on the Conglomerate Host.
5. Verify the result: \`ControlURL\` matches the target network and \`Self.DNSName\` from \`tailscale status --json\` has exactly the chosen first label and the correct suffix. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback: node ID, machine key, final name, owner and IPv4/IPv6. A \`-1\` suffix or another name is not success: resolve a collision or an earlier manual rename with the administrator; do not delete another node. Do not rename an approved device just for convention: its name is part of exact access bindings, so a change needs a coordinated plan and new verification. Treat re-enrollment changing the node ID or machine key the same way.
6. Tailnet registration grants no access yet. For a work or Team Environment, the Organization Admin approves the exact device and its assigned path through the network administrator's managed procedure. No Organization Admin approves a device for a personal Environment: a reviewed change by the Conglomerate Host owner admits it in the home tailnet. Each computer has its own binding and SSH key; never copy a private key. The administrator compares fresh Headscale readback from step 5 for both computers and their exact network bindings; do not assume a device list in the Organization overview. Only after approval and deployment, if the target Environment offers SSH, continue in its Settings → SSH from your devices, pin its host key and verify \`ssh <alias> true\`. A personal Environment without tailnet or SSH, entered only by signing in with the Lazurio account on the web, has no such path. Distinguish registration, approval, deployment and the test; say what is missing.

## How the user works with you
The user sits at their own computer and works with you in the clients they prefer, possibly several at once: ChatGPT Desktop (formerly Codex Desktop) over SSH, T3 Code on the web or in its desktop app, and Lazurio MausBot. The handover does not record where the user connects from. Your harness tells you which client you run in; the user may be working in another one meanwhile. Your shell, files and processes are here, not on the user's computer.

- **Previews.** \`localhost\` and \`127.0.0.1\` exist only here; never write a bare link to them into the chat.
  - When you run in ChatGPT Desktop over SSH, open work in progress in its built-in browser. Current versions open an SSH tunnel to the port themselves, on a random port of the user's computer. OpenAI does not document this and older versions cannot do it, so check that the page really loaded.
  - When you run in T3 Code or Lazurio MausBot, no port reaches the user. Start a module's application in progress (\`lazurio module start\`) and send its \`runtime.url\`; hand over any other page as a file.
- **Links.** \`lazurio chat link\` prints the link into this Environment's T3 Code.
- **Files from the user.** A file dragged into the ChatGPT Desktop chat arrives in \`~/.codex/attachments/\`; into T3 Code and Lazurio MausBot the user uploads it in the chat. Save what you will need later in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`.
- **Files for the user.** Save finished files in \`~/Documents/<task>/\`. The user has no direct access to this Environment's files and a \`/home/…\` path opens nothing on their computer, so hand over the result the way their client can: show an image in the chat, open a document in ChatGPT Desktop's preview. A large file (tens of MB) does not reliably pass through the preview; say so and offer a smaller or split version.
- **Chats.** Conversations from every client are stored here. The user deletes and archives chats in their own client; do not delete session files yourself. In ChatGPT Desktop, a thread's menu (right-click the thread in the sidebar) offers Permanently delete only in Codex mode (the switcher at the top left); in ChatGPT mode it offers only Archive. When someone asks how to delete a thread, tell them.

## Enabled tools
The CLIs of the Lazurio catalog that agents in this Environment are to use: the required ones and those the user enabled in this Folder (\`lazurio tools list\`, \`lazurio tools enable|disable <tool>\`). Enabling is context, not authority or installation: it grants no access, installs nothing and pins no version. Report a missing or signed-out tool to the user and install or sign it in only with their consent; offer them a newer version (\`manual/troubleshooting.md\`).

- \`gh\` (required): GitHub CLI for work with repositories, pull requests, issues and reviews. Use \`gh\` for all GitHub work. Verify the signed-in identity with \`gh auth status\` before a connected operation; a sign-in is not proof of the right to an exact operation.
- \`executor\` (required): This Environment's MCP gateway for directly connected Integrations and custom MCP servers; it runs as a service on localhost only and Lazurio looks after it. Use the Integrations connected directly through the MCP server \`executor\` (its tools \`skills\` and \`execute\`) or with \`executor call tools <integration> <owner> <connection> <tool> '<json>'\`; \`executor tools integrations\` and \`executor tools search "<task>"\` show what is there. Check the service and the agents' connection with \`lazurio executor status\`; when it reports that Executor is not running or not finished, tell the user: on their instruction \`lazurio executor setup\` repairs it (Settings → Tools → executor offers the same). An externally visible write through an Integration (sending, publishing, deleting) is a Publication: prepare the work and leave it unfinished; the user's explicit “Publish” for it is the full mandate to finish it (\`manual/working-here.md\`).

### Integrations and MCP
The Integrations (connected apps) and custom MCP servers of this Environment are held by the Environment's Executor and by Composio with the user's account; \`lazurio integrations list --json\` prints what is connected and which way, and the rules are in \`manual/working-here.md\` (section Integrations). Integrations are never recorded in the Folder, and neither a connected app nor an available MCP server is consent to Publication.

## Connecting a computer and a ChatGPT Desktop project
The agent on the user's computer sets up the connection, not you. You are in the Environment the computer connects to, and the computer's private key does not belong here.
The handover gives no Launchpad address, so do not guess it. Ask the Environment's administrator to add it.
After a successful SSH test, the agent on the source computer ends its chat reply with a clickable Markdown link to ChatGPT Desktop taken exactly from the page. It does not add the project itself or bypass app security restrictions: the user clicks. Explain that if the first click adds no project, they can click the same link once again; this sometimes happens and is not a required double-click. Once successful, the user sees the Lazurio folder in the left sidebar. A single Organization project may simply be named after the Organization; distinguish several Environments of one Organization by kind as well, such as work and Team, and give a personal project its own personal label. Only the display name changes, never the SSH alias or remote path. If the link still fails, guide the user through adding the page's exact values manually in Settings → Connections → SSH.
A registered key gives no network access by itself. The network administrator decides which devices reach the network (Headscale and the SSH grant). Do not switch the Tailscale profile on the user's computer.

## SSH to other Environments and devices
- Connect only to a peer's tailnet hostname as the handover records it (the \`ssh.host\` of a peer above), never to a bare tailnet address such as \`100.64.0.x\`: those addresses repeat in every tailnet and mean a different peer in each.
- Before connecting, verify the active tailnet: \`tailscale status --json\` → \`CurrentTailnet.Name\` must be this Environment's home tailnet and \`CurrentTailnet.MagicDNSSuffix\` must match the peers' recorded \`ssh.host\` names. If it does not, or you cannot tell, stop and tell the user. Never switch the Tailscale profile: it is one global setting for every session in this Environment (the section Boundaries in this Environment above).
- The peer's host key is always pinned: in \`~/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio\` under the peer's name as \`HostKeyAlias\`. With the values from the handover:

  \`\`\`sh
  PEER='peer-name'                  # the peer's name
  SSH_HOST='peer-name.tailnet.host' # its ssh.host
  TARGET="$SSH_HOST"               # or "account@$SSH_HOST" when the handover names ssh.user
  ssh -o HostKeyAlias="$PEER" -o UserKnownHostsFile="$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts_lazurio" -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes "$TARGET"
  \`\`\`

- The pinned key comes from whoever owns the peer (the records of the Organization that owns it, the user for their own device), never from the first connection: no \`ssh-keyscan\`, no accepting an unknown key, never the general \`~/.ssh/known_hosts\`. Without a pinned key, do not connect; ask the user for it.
- “Host key changed” (\`REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED\`) first means “a different active tailnet”, never a reason to remove or accept the key again. Stop and report it.
- A listed peer is transport Headscale allows, not permission for the task: the SSH key, the account and the user's mandate stay separate.
- Lazurio enforces nothing about what this Environment can reach: the Headscale policy of the Conglomerate Host does. This manual only repeats what the handover recorded; never infer a peer the handover does not list from names or addresses.

## From this personal Remote Environment
The handover records no peer this Environment may reach over SSH; do not look for one, tell the user.
- Reachability is decided by Headscale and is neither identity nor mandate: the handover does not record whose a peer is. Before you work on a peer or operate a device, confirm with the user that it is theirs or assigned to them.
- Organization work (code, repositories, runs) belongs in a work Remote Environment the user confirms is assigned to them, never in this Environment: connect over SSH and work there. Never clone an Organization repository into this personal Remote Environment.
- What needs the user's own device, for example the signed-in browser, is done over SSH on a device the user confirms is theirs.
- No work Environment ever reaches this personal Remote Environment (decision 0155); do not open a way back.
"
`;

